    "answerText": "Randolph threatened to organize 100,000 Black workers to march on Washington unless the federal government banned racial discrimination in wartime defense hiring. Roosevelt signed Executive Order 8802 on June 25, 1941 — making it the most significant federal civil rights action since Reconstruction.",
    "subject": "A. Philip Randolph and the March That Never Happened",
    "dailyStory": "<p>In 1941, the United States was gearing up for war. Defense factories were hiring by the thousands. Black workers applied and got turned away. The jobs were for white workers only.</p>\n<p>A. Philip Randolph had been fighting this exact fight for decades. He had spent sixteen years building the Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters into the first major Black labor union in the country. He understood exactly how power worked.</p>\n<p>So he made a threat.</p>\n<p>He told President Franklin Roosevelt that if the government did not desegregate wartime defense industries, he would organize 100,000 Black Americans to march on Washington. This was 1941 — twenty-two years before the famous March on Washington, when Dr. King delivered &quot;I Have a Dream.&quot; Randolph invented the idea.</p>\n<p>Roosevelt panicked. A march of that size would be an international embarrassment while America was preparing to fight fascism abroad. The president negotiated. Randolph did not move.</p>\n<p>On June 25, 1941, Roosevelt signed Executive Order 8802 — the first federal law banning racial discrimination in defense work since Reconstruction. No march ever happened.</p>\n<p>Because it didn't have to.</p>\n<p>Randolph understood something most people miss: the threat of organized power is sometimes more useful than the power itself. He had built sixteen years of real union strength before making that threat. Roosevelt knew the march was real. That's why the order got signed.</p>",
    "waitWhat": "<p>A. Philip Randolph threatened to march 100,000 Black workers on Washington in 1941 — and then called it off when Roosevelt agreed to desegregate defense industries.</p>\n<p>The march never happened.</p>\n<p>But it didn't need to. The threat was real because the union was real. Randolph had spent sixteen years building the Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters into a genuine political force. Roosevelt knew he could actually do it.</p>\n<p>Twenty-two years later, Randolph helped organize the actual March on Washington. He stood at the podium in 1963 at age 74. The idea he had been developing since 1941 finally happened on the largest scale in American history.</p>\n<p>He had been building toward that day for over two decades.</p>",
    "costImpact": "<p>Randolph spent twelve years fighting the Pullman Company — the largest private employer of Black workers in America — before winning the Brotherhood's first labor contract in 1937. The company had every advantage. Randolph kept organizing anyway.</p>\n<p>The FBI surveilled him for decades. He was labeled a radical and called a communist for arguing that Black workers deserved the same wages and rights as white workers. During World War I, federal agents briefly arrested him for his antiwar views.</p>\n<p>The impact was permanent. Executive Order 8802 opened wartime defense industries to Black workers who had been locked out. Executive Order 9981 ended segregation in the military — a change that affected millions of Black Americans in the decades that followed. The March on Washington, which Randolph helped design and lead, is part of the permanent record of American democratic protest.</p>\n<p>He built the infrastructure that made each of these victories possible before any of them happened.</p>",
    "whyItMatters": "<p>Randolph demonstrated that organized power could force federal action even when the law was not on your side. His model — build real institutions, make credible demands, refuse to move — became the blueprint for modern civil rights strategy. The March on Washington of 1963, which defined the Civil Rights Movement for generations, was an idea he first proposed in 1941 and used as leverage before it ever took place.</p>",
