    "answer": 2,
    "answerText": "Carter G. Woodson established Negro History Week in February 1926, choosing the second week of the month to coincide with the birthdays of Frederick Douglass and Abraham Lincoln.",
    "subject": "Carter G. Woodson and the Creation of Negro History Week",
    "dailyStory": "<p>Here's what they didn't teach you: Black history wasn't left out of textbooks by accident. It was left out on purpose.</p>\n<p>Carter G. Woodson figured that out the hard way. He grew up poor in Virginia, taught himself to read as a kid, and eventually earned a PhD from Harvard — one of only two Black Americans who had ever done that. Then he opened a history textbook. And he couldn't find himself or his people in it.</p>\n<p>So he decided to fix it himself.</p>\n<p>In 1926, Woodson created <em>Negro History Week</em> — a single week in February, timed to the birthdays of Frederick Douglass and Abraham Lincoln, because he knew schools were already talking about those two men. His plan: get Black history into a conversation that was already happening, then expand from there.</p>\n<p>It worked. Schools started teaching it. Communities started celebrating it. And over the next fifty years, that one week grew into something nobody could ignore.</p>\n<p>In 1976 — fifty years after Woodson started it — the United States officially recognized Black History Month.</p>\n<p>He didn't live to see it. But he built everything it stands on.</p>",
    "waitWhat": "<p>Carter G. Woodson did not create Negro History Week because Black history was missing by accident.</p>\n<p>He created it because Black history was being left out on purpose.</p>\n<p>Schools taught American history while ignoring Black soldiers, inventors, writers, teachers, leaders, and communities that helped build the country. Woodson saw the missing pages and decided to make the country read them.</p>",
    "costImpact": "<p>Woodson spent his life building institutions that should have already existed. He had to create an association, launch a journal, publish books, and organize a national history week because schools and publishers refused to treat Black history as essential.</p>\n<p>That work took money, time, credibility, and constant effort. He died in 1950, twenty-six years before Negro History Week officially became Black History Month. He never got to see the full national recognition of what he built.</p>",
    "whyItMatters": "<p>Without Woodson's deliberate institutional work, Black history would have remained absent from school curricula and public consciousness for generations longer. He didn't wait for the system to change — he built the infrastructure himself: the association, the journal, the annual observance. Every February, that infrastructure operates, whether or not his name is attached to it.</p>",
