    "desc": "Born in Canada to parents who escaped slavery, Elijah McCoy trained as a mechanical engineer in Scotland and invented the automatic lubrication system that kept America's railroads running — inspiring the phrase 'the real McCoy.'",
    "question": "<p>What did Elijah McCoy invent that railroad engineers insisted on having over any imitation?</p>",
    "options": [
      "A more efficient steam boiler design",
      "An automatic lubrication system for engines in motion",
      "A railroad signal switching device",
      "An improved steam brake mechanism"
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "answerText": "McCoy invented an automatic lubricator that fed oil to an engine's moving parts while the train was still in motion. His version worked so much better than imitations that engineers began demanding \"the real McCoy\" before accepting any substitution — a phrase still used today.",
    "subject": "Elijah McCoy and the Invention of Automatic Lubrication",
    "dailyStory": "<p>The railroad ran on steam, and steam engines needed oil. But in 1866, you had to stop the train to oil the engine — every eighty miles or so, the whole machine halted while workers climbed underneath and lubricated the moving parts by hand. Trains waited. Schedules broke. Money was lost.</p>\n<p>Elijah McCoy had a better idea.</p>\n<p>He had grown up in Canada, the son of parents who escaped slavery in Kentucky through the Underground Railroad. He trained as a mechanical engineer in Edinburgh, Scotland. Then he came to Michigan looking for engineering work — and found that no railroad would hire a Black engineer.</p>\n<p>So they gave him a fireman's job: shoveling coal and oiling engines by hand.</p>\n<p>While he was doing the work the system said was all he deserved, he was thinking about how to fix the problem.</p>\n<p>In 1872, McCoy patented his first automatic lubricator — a device that fed oil to engine components through a system of tubes while the train was still moving. No more stops. No more delays. Just continuous movement and a machine that ran better.</p>\n<p>Railroad engineers learned quickly: McCoy's lubricator worked better than every imitation. They started demanding &quot;the real McCoy&quot; before accepting a shipment.</p>\n<p>That phrase is still used today to mean the genuine article, not a substitute. Most people using it have no idea where it came from.</p>",
    "waitWhat": "<p>Elijah McCoy trained as a mechanical engineer in Scotland. He came to America with credentials the railroad industry refused to recognize because he was Black.</p>\n<p>So they gave him a job oiling engines by hand.</p>\n<p>He used that job to study exactly what the machines needed — and invented a device that lubricated them automatically while they were still moving. His invention worked so much better than the imitations that engineers began demanding &quot;the real McCoy&quot; before accepting any substitute.</p>\n<p>That phrase outlasted him. Most people who use it today have no idea they are quoting the name of a Black inventor whose skills his employers refused to acknowledge — a man who built his laboratory out of the job they said was all he deserved.</p>",
    "costImpact": "<p>McCoy held 57 patents by the time he died, but he earned recognition long after the inventions had already made other people wealthy. Patent ownership and manufacturing profit were two different things, and McCoy spent his career on the inventor's side of that divide. The Elijah McCoy Manufacturing Company he founded in 1920 struggled financially despite the value of what he had built.</p>\n<p>The impact of his work extended far beyond the railroad industry. Automatic lubrication principles he developed were adapted for factory machinery, marine engines, and eventually automotive systems. The ability to keep machines running without stopping for maintenance was one of the core enablers of continuous industrial production in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. McCoy's 1872 patent addressed a real problem that was limiting American industry, and he solved it in a machine shop while working as a fireman.</p>\n<p>The State of Michigan and city of Detroit eventually honored him with historical recognition. His name, preserved in everyday language, is used millions of times a day by people who have never heard of him.</p>",
    "whyItMatters": "<p>McCoy's story is the story of Black innovation under constraint. He had the credentials, the training, and the talent to work as an engineer. The system refused him. Instead of leaving the field, he turned the job they gave him into the research he needed to solve a problem the industry hadn't solved. His inventions shaped American industrial production for generations. The phrase he inspired remains in everyday use. And the name attached to it — the real McCoy — is his.</p>",
