    "desc": "In May 1961, interracial groups of civil rights activists rode interstate buses through the South to enforce a Supreme Court ruling against segregated bus terminals — and were met with fire, beatings, and mass arrests that forced the federal government to act.",
    "question": "<p>What were the Freedom Riders trying to enforce when they rode interstate buses through the South in May 1961?</p>",
    "options": [
      "A federal voting rights law requiring equal registration access",
      "A Supreme Court ruling that segregated interstate bus terminals were unconstitutional",
      "An executive order from President Kennedy desegregating transportation",
      "A provision of the 14th Amendment requiring equal access to public facilities"
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "answerText": "The Freedom Riders were testing enforcement of Boynton v. Virginia (1960), in which the Supreme Court ruled that segregation in interstate bus terminals — waiting rooms, lunch counters, and restrooms — was unconstitutional. The ruling existed; the South was ignoring it. The rides were designed to force the federal government to enforce its own court's decision.",
    "subject": "The Freedom Riders and the Fight to Enforce the Law",
    "dailyStory": "<p>The law was already on their side.</p>\n<p>The Supreme Court had ruled in 1960 that segregated interstate bus terminals were unconstitutional. Black and white passengers were legally entitled to sit anywhere on an interstate bus and use the same waiting rooms, restrooms, and lunch counters at any terminal along the route.</p>\n<p>The South ignored the ruling.</p>\n<p>In May 1961, the Congress of Racial Equality sent thirteen riders — seven Black, six white — onto interstate buses heading south from Washington, D.C. Their plan was deliberate: sit where the law said they could sit, use the facilities the law said they could use, and see what happened.</p>\n<p>What happened was violence.</p>\n<p>In Anniston, Alabama, a mob surrounded the bus and firebombed it. Riders who escaped were beaten as they fled. In Birmingham, a mob attacked them with baseball bats and chains while police stood back for fifteen minutes. In Montgomery, they were beaten again.</p>\n<p>The Kennedy administration pressured the Congress of Racial Equality to stop the rides.</p>\n<p>CORE refused. More riders joined — including Diane Nash and John Lewis, who were 22 and 21 years old. By the end of the summer, more than 400 Freedom Riders had been arrested in Mississippi, filling jails and prison cells they refused to leave on bail.</p>\n<p>In November 1961, the federal government issued regulations enforcing desegregation across all interstate transportation.</p>\n<p>The law had existed for a year. The Freedom Riders made the country enforce it.</p>",
    "waitWhat": "<p>The Freedom Riders did not discover that interstate bus segregation was illegal.</p>\n<p>The Supreme Court had already ruled it was illegal in 1960.</p>\n<p>They boarded those buses because the ruling was being ignored. The federal government had a court decision and wasn't enforcing it. The South was operating as if nothing had changed.</p>\n<p>Riders were firebombed in Anniston, beaten in Birmingham, beaten again in Montgomery, and arrested in Jackson — all while following the law. The violence was committed by people trying to maintain illegal segregation.</p>\n<p>Over 400 riders were arrested for using legally desegregated facilities. They refused bail and filled the jails.</p>\n<p>In November 1961, the federal government finally issued enforcement regulations.</p>\n<p>The law already existed. The Freedom Riders made the country mean it.</p>",
    "costImpact": "<p>Thirteen riders became hundreds. Every arrest in Mississippi was calculated — riders knew they were going to jail and boarded the buses anyway. Some were beaten so severely they required hospitalization. Some bore physical injuries for the rest of their lives. John Lewis, beaten in South Carolina and again in Montgomery, went on to serve in Congress for over three decades. He bore the scars of the movement.</p>\n<p>The Kennedy administration was embarrassed internationally. The United States was presenting itself as the leader of the free world — opposing Soviet communism in the name of freedom and democracy — while television showed Americans beating civil rights workers and firebombing buses in Alabama. That pressure from international opinion, amplified by news photographs and television coverage, accelerated federal action.</p>\n<p>The Interstate Commerce Commission regulations issued on November 1, 1961, required the removal of all segregation signs and the integration of all waiting rooms, lunch counters, and restrooms in interstate transportation terminals. The enforcement mechanism now existed where none had before.</p>\n<p>More than 400 Freedom Riders were arrested to make that happen.</p>",
    "whyItMatters": "<p>The Freedom Rides demonstrated that legal victories alone do not produce change — someone has to enforce them, or force them to be enforced. The riders used their willingness to absorb violence and arrest to convert a Supreme Court ruling into actual practice. Their model — deliberate, nonviolent confrontation designed to expose injustice by provoking a response — became central to civil rights strategy throughout the 1960s. They showed that the gap between law and reality could be forced closed, but only by people willing to stand in that gap.</p>",
