    "answer": 2,
    "answerText": "\"Say It Loud — I'm Black and I'm Proud,\" released in August 1968, was a deliberate political statement at a time when calling yourself \"Black\" — rather than \"Negro\" or \"colored\" — was itself a declaration. The song reached number one on the R&B charts despite resistance from radio stations.",
    "subject": "James Brown and the Sound of Black Pride",
    "dailyStory": "<p>The night Martin Luther King Jr. was shot — April 4, 1968 — cities across America started burning.</p>\n<p>James Brown was scheduled to perform in Boston the next day. City officials asked him to cancel the show. He said no. He would perform, but he wanted it televised live so people could watch from home instead of taking to the streets.</p>\n<p>The mayor agreed. The governor agreed.</p>\n<p>That night, while riots broke out in over 100 American cities, Boston mostly stayed quiet. Historians and city officials have credited the broadcast — and Brown's presence — as a factor that kept the city calm.</p>\n<p>This was not an accident. James Brown had been thinking about the relationship between music and Black political life his entire career.</p>\n<p>He had come from extreme poverty in South Carolina, dropped out of school, shined shoes and danced for pennies, and served time in a reform school as a teenager. He got out through music. Then he spent thirty years building an entertainment empire with his own record label, his own publishing company, and his own touring operation — traveling up to 330 nights a year.</p>\n<p>In 1968, while the country was burning, he made a record called &quot;Say It Loud — I'm Black and I'm Proud.&quot; Some radio stations refused to play it.</p>\n<p>It became a hit anyway.</p>\n<p>Brown never separated music from politics. He understood that saying something out loud — in a song, to a crowd, over a television broadcast — was itself a political act.</p>",
    "waitWhat": "<p>The night after Martin Luther King Jr. was assassinated, James Brown performed a live concert in Boston — but had it televised so people would stay home and watch rather than go into the streets.</p>\n<p>Cities across America were burning.</p>\n<p>Boston didn't.</p>\n<p>Brown used music as a deliberate tool to hold a city together on one of the most volatile nights in modern American history. He made a strategic decision to perform rather than cancel, broadcast rather than contain, and trust the audience rather than fear them.</p>\n<p>It worked. And he knew what he was doing.</p>",
    "costImpact": "<p>Brown grew up in poverty severe enough that he was turned away from school for lacking adequate clothing. He served time in a reform school at 16. Everything he built, he built without a safety net — and once he built it, he used it.</p>\n<p>He invested his platform deliberately. The Boston concert the night after King's assassination was not a routine performance — it was a calculated use of his public trust at an extraordinary moment. &quot;Say It Loud — I'm Black and I'm Proud&quot; was recorded during a period when Black self-identification was politically charged, and radio stations that refused to play it were making a political statement by their refusal.</p>\n<p>The musical impact is structural. Brown's rhythmic innovations — the emphasis on the downbeat, the use of horns and guitars as percussion, the layered polyrhythmic patterns — defined what popular music sounds like in America. Hip-hop, which emerged in the 1970s, built itself on James Brown samples. Hundreds of the most sampled recordings in music history are his. His influence is not historical — it is present in nearly every genre of contemporary American music made today.</p>\n<p>He structured Black pride into the music itself so that anyone who danced to it was participating in it.</p>",
    "whyItMatters": "<p>Brown demonstrated that cultural production was political action. His 1968 concert in Boston showed that a performer with genuine community trust could hold a city together on a night when others were burning. His recordings gave language and rhythm to Black pride at a moment when both were needed. The innovations he invented in the 1960s are still the structural foundation of American popular music, heard in every song that follows the patterns he built.</p>",
