    "subject": "James Meredith and the March Against Fear",
    "dailyStory": "<p>Imagine deciding to walk 220 miles across Mississippi by yourself.</p>\n<p>Not with thousands of supporters.</p>\n<p>Not with television cameras following every step.</p>\n<p>Alone.</p>\n<p>That was James Meredith's plan in June 1966.</p>\n<p>Meredith wanted to prove that a Black citizen could travel through Mississippi without fear. Four years earlier he had become the first Black student admitted to the University of Mississippi, surviving riots, threats, and constant federal protection.</p>\n<p>Now he wanted to test whether the state had really changed.</p>\n<p>It hadn't.</p>\n<p>On the second day of the march, a white sniper stepped out from the trees and shot Meredith.</p>\n<p>Most people would have quit.</p>\n<p>Instead, while Meredith recovered in the hospital, civil rights leaders continued the march in his name. Thousands joined. When he was healthy enough to walk again, Meredith returned and completed the final miles himself.</p>\n<p>The man who tried to stop the march only made it larger.</p>",
    "waitWhat": "<p>James Meredith started the March Against Fear alone on purpose.</p>\n<p>He wanted to prove that a Black citizen could walk through Mississippi without being terrorized. On the second day, he was shot by a white sniper on the side of the road.</p>\n<p>Then, after recovering in the hospital, Meredith came back and finished the march.</p>\n<p>The man who tried to stop him only made the march bigger.</p>",
    "costImpact": "<p>Meredith paid a physical and emotional price for challenging segregation. At Ole Miss, he needed federal protection just to attend class. During the March Against Fear, he was shot in the head, neck, and back.</p>\n<p>His courage also exposed the danger Black citizens faced when trying to vote, travel, or simply move freely. The attack brought national attention to Mississippi and helped push thousands of Black citizens to register to vote.</p>",
